'''Islam in Guajicutea''' is the island's Muslim tradition. Almost all Guajicutean Muslims are [[Sunni Islam|Sunni]], and nearly all trace their religious background to [[Bayani]] immigration from [[Balisca]] and the wider Bayani diaspora. In Guajicutea, the religion has remained closely associated with Bayani artisan and labour communities, and it has generally followed mainstream Sunni practice rather than developing a strongly local doctrinal split.

Islam in Guajicutea is socially visible but numerically smaller than [[Christianity in Guajicutea|Petran Catholicism]]. As of 2025, it encompasses about 284,000 residents, almost all of them Sunni and almost all tracing their religious background to Bayani migration from [[Balisca]] and the wider Bayani diaspora. It is concentrated in urban and port districts, in older Bayani neighbourhoods, and in some inland labour settlements. The community is historically linked to household worship, Qur'anic recitation, mutual-aid networks, and neighbourhood solidarity, all of which helped it survive periods of legal restriction and open hostility.

== History ==

=== Bayani migration and early settlement ===

Islam first took root in Guajicutea through Bayani migration from Balisca after the island came under Sartan colonial rule. Bayani migrants were welcomed mainly as labourers, field workers, dock workers, artisans, and small traders, especially in the colonial and early republican economies. Their communities remained socially distinct, and their religious life was shaped by migration, work, and family networks rather than by state patronage.

During the colonial period and much of the first republican era, Bayani Muslims faced restrictions on land ownership, public worship, and mosque building. The Sartan colonial state and the Petran Catholic establishment both treated them as a socially separate population. In practice, prayer was usually done at home, in rented rooms, or in quiet neighborhood gatherings. Public congregational life was limited, and Muslim worship remained mostly domestic.

=== First Republic and the first mosque ===

The first major change came during the Republic of Guajicutea. In 1805, after rising tension around Bayani worship and local hostility in the port districts, the Bishop of Tabakurí and President [[Mateo Kurí]] issued a joint edict to calm the violence and recognise the right of Bayani households to worship without harassment. That same year, the first mosque in Guajicutea was built in [[Warikú]]. Because vandalism remained a concern, the community had to hire local security to protect the building and its worshippers.

Even then, the mosque did not mark full equality. It was the first visible Muslim institution on the island, but it existed in a political climate that still treated Bayani Muslims as socially suspect. The mosque remained a focal point for the community, yet much of religious life still took place in homes and family compounds.

=== Northern Confederation occupation ===

The [[Northern Confederation]] occupation of 1812 brought a sharp reversal. The occupation regime imposed racial codes, restricted movement, and pushed Bayani Muslims into segregated urban quarters and the hinterland. The mosque in Warikú was destroyed during this period, and public Muslim institutions were suppressed. In response, Bayani Muslims created a secret domestic religious order, [[Jamaat al-Hifaz]] (Protection Society), to organise mutual defence, maintain religious discipline, and preserve community networks under repression.

Jamaat al-Hifaz remained clandestine for more than a century. It acted as a combination of prayer circle, aid network, and self-protection group. During later political crises, especially in the 1930s, its surviving networks came into contact with the broader [[Guarda Revolutionary]] tradition and the island's liberty movements.

=== Second Republic ===

After the [[Virtuous Revolution]] and the restoration of self-government, Bayani Muslims regained formal legal protection. The second republican order was more tolerant than the occupation regime, but it still did not allow new mosques to be built for much of the period. As a result, Muslim practice remained partly hidden in domestic and neighbourhood spaces, even though the state no longer imposed explicit anti-Muslim laws.

Social discrimination also remained strong. Bayani Muslims were still often identified with low-status labour, and their communities were treated as outsiders by many Petran Catholic elites. At the same time, the religion became more deeply rooted in Guajicutean life. Intermarriage increased, especially between Bayani and Kaftian families in urban districts, and some Kaftians adopted Islam as a rejection of slavery, racism, and the church-backed social order.

A notable expression of that trend appeared in 1903, when the Kaftian Guaji writer [[Yahya Kaftí]] published a defence of his conversion to Islam. His writing denounced what he called the slave religion and argued that Islam offered discipline, dignity, and moral equality to people who had been excluded by both church and class hierarchy.

=== Great Adonian War and postwar changes ===

The [[Great Adonian War]] and the postwar settlement brought greater recognition of Muslim rights. After 1947, Muslims could practise openly, establish institutions more freely, and claim a clearer place in the country's religious life. The postwar constitution did not make Islam a state religion, but it did protect freedom of worship and community autonomy.

From this period onward, Guajicutean Muslims became more openly visible in public life. Most communities remained conservative in social outlook, and Muslim voters often supported parties and candidates that promised order, family security, property stability, and religious tolerance. That conservative reputation became especially visible in modern politics, including the Bayani-led protests against the recognition of same-sex marriage in 2018.

== Belief and practice ==

Guajicutean Islam follows general Sunni standards. Most communities observe daily prayer, fasting during Ramadan, almsgiving, and the major life-cycle rites associated with Sunni practice. Religious teaching is usually straightforward and orthodox, with little interest in local doctrinal innovation. The island's Muslim communities have never been large enough to develop a major separate school of law or theology, so their religious identity has remained close to broader Sunni norms.

At the same time, local social history has shaped the community's institutions. Because Bayani Muslims were long concentrated in artisan and labour households, the religion developed a strong culture of mutual aid, apprenticeship ties, and household teaching. The home remained the most important religious space for much of the community's history, especially during periods when mosques were restricted or destroyed.

== Community life ==

In everyday Guajicutean life, Muslim communities are most visible in neighbourhood networks, family gatherings, and public festivals around Eid. Food, dress, and family discipline tend to follow standard Sunni expectations, while community schools and religious instruction are often organised through household or neighbourhood arrangements. In many districts, Bayani Muslim families live near each other and maintain dense social ties across work, marriage, and worship.

The religion also remains tied to class identity. Historically, Bayani Muslims were seen as artisans, dock workers, and labourers, and that association still shapes the way many Guajicuteans describe the community. In modern politics, Muslims are often viewed as one of the island's more socially conservative constituencies, though that tendency should not be mistaken for a single party alignment.

